  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nAn upper level trough is currently traversing across the \nnortheastern CONUS with an upper low moving onshore to the Pacific \nNorthwest with upper ridging between these two features. At the \nsurface, a low sits over the border of Alberta and Saskatchewan with \nsurface troughing extending down through the high Plains. In our \narea, southerly winds have started to increase as the pressure \ngradient continues to tighten from lee troughing. The pressure \ngradient will keep strengthening into Saturday as the upper trough \ndigs into the western CONUS, resulting in breezy southerly winds \nacross our area. This pattern will persist through Monday with \nsustained winds between 15-25 mph and gusts up to 30-35 mph each day \nwith the strongest winds generally west of the KS Turnpike. Well \nabove normal high temperatures are forecast Friday through Tuesday \nas increased thickness and above normal 850 mb temperatures persist. \nHighs are expected to be in the upper 80s each day with some \nlocations reaching into the lower 90s, especially on Monday. \n\nA pattern change is likely to start on Tuesday-Tuesday night as the \naforementioned upper trough over the western CONUS propagates east \nand approaches the Rockies into Wednesday. This trough will bring \nthe next round of shower and storm chances to the region especially \nas a cold front is expected to push south through the central Plains \ninto Kansas by Tuesday morning. These rain chances will be possible \nfrom Tuesday to Thursday before the trough continues to slide east \non Friday. Strong to severe storms may be possible with this \nactivity as effective wind shear increases combined with ample \ninstability and forcing from the trough and cold front. Heavy \nrainfall appears likely as well as PWATs approach 2\" (greater than \nthe maximum for this time of year) across our area. Details on \ntiming, location and impacts will continue to be refined as we get \ncloser. From mid to late week, temperatures are forecast to become \nmore seasonable as highs return to the 70s. Guidance suggests the \npotential for below normal temperatures heading into next weekend \nwith highs in the 50s to 60s and lows in the 40s. Stay tuned!",
